            "text": "Takes the largest value in each window of values: windows side by side shrink a sequence (or an image) while keeping its peaks; a window sliding one value at a time holds every peak for the length of the window.\n\nShrinking while keeping peaks is what pooling layers do inside networks, and what you want when reducing a fast stream - a hit, a spike or a sudden movement survives, where averaging would blur it away. Sliding, it is a running maximum: the loudest of the last eight frames, a simple envelope that rises at once and falls only when the peak leaves the window. The answer stays on the input's device.\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.max_pool <window> <step>\nDefaults: window 2, step the same as the window (windows side by side).\n\nEXAMPLE:\nt.max_pool 4\nt.max_pool 8 1\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\ntensor in:\nThe values (a list or tensor). Every input is pooled and sent.\n\nwindow (int box, also an inlet):\nHow many values each window holds (each side of a tile, over two dims).\n\nstep (int box, also an inlet):\nHow far each window moves on from the last. The same as the window (the default) takes windows side by side, with no overlap: the length shrinks by that factor. 1 slides the window one value at a time: one result per position - the running maximum.\n\ndim (int box, also an inlet):\nWhich dim to pool along: -1 (the default) along each row, 0 down each column - over time, for a buffer of frames x joints.\n\npool over:\nAn option, under the node. one dim (the default), or last two dims - square tiles of an image or grid, window x window, every image of a stack at once.\n\npartial window:\nAn option, under the node. Off (the default), values left over at the end that do not fill a window are dropped. On, they make one last, shorter window.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\npooled:\nThe largest value of each window, the shape of the input with the pooled dim shortened.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nTop: 1 5 3 8 2 2 9 0 6 in windows of 3 side by side - 1 5 3, 8 2 2, 9 0 6 - gives the three peaks, 5 8 9. Bottom: the same window sliding one value at a time gives 5 8 8 8 9 9 9: each peak held while it stays in the window.\n\nRELATED:\nt.avg_pool takes the mean instead. t.unfold gives every window itself; t.max along a dim, or t.cummax for the running maximum from the start."
        },
